# Deployment Instructions

## Prerequisites

- `kubectl` installed and configured to connect to your cluster
- Access to the `mateapp` namespace (or cluster-admin rights to create it)

---

## Deployment Order

> **Important:** follow this order strictly — the todoapp Service must exist before the DaemonSet and CronJob are applied, otherwise all `curl` calls will fail with DNS resolution errors.

### Step 1 — Create the namespace

```bash
kubectl create namespace mateapp
```

### Step 2 — Deploy the TodoApp Pod and ClusterIP Service

```bash
kubectl apply -f todoapp.yml
```

Wait until the Pod is `Running`:

```bash
kubectl get pod todoapp -n mateapp
```

Verify the Service was created:

```bash
kubectl get service todoapp -n mateapp
```

Expected output:
```
NAME       TYPE        CLUSTER-IP      EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)   AGE
todoapp    ClusterIP   10.x.x.x        <none>        80/TCP    10s
```

### Step 3 — Deploy the DaemonSet

```bash
kubectl apply -f daemonset.yml
```

### Step 4 — Deploy the CronJob

```bash
kubectl apply -f cronjob.yml
```

---

## Validation

### Validate TodoApp

Confirm the Pod is running and the Service is reachable:

```bash
kubectl get pod todoapp -n mateapp
kubectl get service todoapp -n mateapp
```

### Validate DaemonSet

Check that a pod is scheduled on every node:

```bash
kubectl get daemonset -n mateapp
kubectl get pods -n mateapp -l app=todoapp-curl-daemonset -o wide
```

Stream logs from a DaemonSet pod — you should see curl output every ~5 seconds:

```bash
kubectl logs -n mateapp -l app=todoapp-curl-daemonset -f
```

Expected output (repeated every 5 seconds):
```
<!DOCTYPE html>...   (or JSON response from todoapp)
```

### Validate CronJob

Check the CronJob status and last schedule time:

```bash
kubectl get cronjob todoapp-health-cronjob -n mateapp
```

List Jobs created by the CronJob (a new one appears every 4 minutes):

```bash
kubectl get jobs -n mateapp
```

View logs from the latest CronJob pod:

```bash
kubectl logs -n mateapp -l app=todoapp-health-cronjob
```

Expected output:
```json
{"status":"ok"}
```

Verify history limits — at most 10 successful and 5 failed jobs are retained:

```bash
kubectl get jobs -n mateapp
```